# Display Stack — external displays, mirroring, topology

Applies when touching `MirrorService.kt`, `DisplayMirrorBackend.kt`,
`DisplayTopologyController.kt`, `DisplayEnvironmentSettings.kt`,
`ResolutionRepository.kt`, `CoverDisplaySessionController.kt`, or anything
that puts pixels on a display other than the phone panel.

**Docs (if stuck):** https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/display/DisplayManager ·
https://developer.android.com/reference/android/view/SurfaceControl

## Invariants

1. **Flutter never renders the desktop.** The desktop is a Dextop-owned
   display (`targetDisplayId`): overlay display from
   `Settings.Global overlay_display_devices`, or privileged
   `IDisplayManager.createVirtualDisplay` bound to a host surface. Flutter
   only drives settings via `MethodChannel`.
2. **External pixels arrive via mirroring.** `DisplayMirrorBackend.attach`
   binds a mirror of the logical desktop to a host `SurfaceView`/layer.
   Three strategies, tried per device profile: `virtual_display`,
   `window_manager.mirrorDisplay`, `surface_control.mirrorDisplay`. Crop and
   letterbox math live in `attachLayer` / `attachCropped` — per-frame
   `SurfaceControl.Transaction` work belongs there.
3. **Extend mode is one logical framebuffer, region-cropped per screen.**
   `extendedDesktopConfig()` unions the regions; `phoneRegion` /
   `externalRegion` are crops in desktop pixels, not separate displays.
4. **External display discovery** = `DisplayManager.DisplayListener` +
   `ExternalDisplayDetector` (type 2/3 or `DisplayAddress.Physical`; never
   overlay type 4 or virtual type 5). `FLAG_PRESENTATION` gates the output pick.
5. **Logical desktop size is free; physical mode is not.** Overlay W×H×dpi
   arbitrary via `requestDisplay` / `ResolutionRepository`; physical display
   modes constrained to `display.supportedModes`.
6. **Density is the app's job on glasses** — XR glasses typically report no
   DPI; Dextop's density override (`wm density -d` / `setDisplayDensity`) is
   what keeps the desktop usable on them.
7. **Privileged operations** (topology get/set, virtual display creation,
   mode forcing, USB grants) go through `PrivilegedAccess` +
   `DistributionPrivilegeRuntime` — never assume adb/root presence.

## Symptom / cause / fix skeleton

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | --- |
| External screen black after resolution change | Phone kept stale DP state across HPD/EDID change | Treat as disconnect/reconnect; re-run attach on next DisplayListener event |
| Mirror stretched/cropped wrong on one device | Strategy mismatch — letterbox math assumed a strategy that fell back | Log which mirror strategy attached; recompute crop for that strategy |
| Desktop tiny (~30 dpi) on glasses display | Glasses report no/low EDID DPI | Apply Dextop density override on attach for XR displays |
| Extend shows phone content on external | Regions swapped or crop empty | Re-derive `phoneRegion`/`externalRegion` from `extendedDesktopConfig()` |
| Phone taps do nothing in every app. Double-tap still wakes. An injected tap can still hit the activity | A virtual desktop in the phone's display group has a focusable trusted overlay, so focus moves there and gesture regions grow to about 10× that desktop. A userspace reader of the touchscreen node (no exclusive grab) feeding a virtual pointer aimed at that desktop turns the finger into a pointer. Clearing reader flags does not close the fd. A gone window does not keep the panel awake, and then every tap is only a wakeup key | Phone-as-phone role only: stop the privileged input engine so the fd closes and the virtual pointer is destroyed, make that shell not focusable and not a touch target, move focus back to the phone display, and hold the panel awake. Topology rediscovery must not restart the reader during that hold. Leave the trackpad role alone. Do not write `overlay_display_devices` to hide the panel |

